<section>
  <p class="kicker">For the ADS-L registry</p>
  <h1>skyBlip applies for AMT entry 58, and for the exception OGN-Tracker has.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    skyBlip builds open-source electronic conspicuity equipment for general
    aviation. The devices transmit and receive <strong>ADS-L 4 SRD-860</strong>
    air-to-air, receive the ADS-L traffic uplink from ground stations, and
    receive FLARM-generation traffic. Under
    <strong>ADS-L.4.SRD860.F.2.2</strong> we apply for <strong>entry 58 of the
    Address Mapping Table</strong>, manufacturers page 49, and we ask that it
    carry the whole 24-bit address with no manufacturer prefix, exactly as
    entry 7 carries OGN-Tracker addresses. A skyBlip would then mint its address
    inside a space that is ours, whether it was shipped by us or built by its
    owner.
  </p>
  <p>
    This page is what accompanies the application to
    <a href="mailto:registry@ads-l.aero">registry@ads-l.aero</a>: who is
    applying, what the equipment does on the air, why the manufacturer prefix of
    F.2.2 does not fit this fleet, and one question we would like the
    registry&rsquo;s view on.
  </p>
</section>

