<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Conformance</h2>
  <h3>The specification is a test suite here, read clause by clause.</h3>

  <p>
    Issue 2 has 80 numbered clauses.
    <a href="https://github.com/fcatuhe/skyblip/tree/main/firmware/test/adsl"><code>firmware/test/adsl/</code></a>
    holds at least one case for each of them, named after the clause it reads,
    and <code>scripts/check_adsl_clauses.py</code> fails the build if a clause
    has no case or if a case names a clause the document does not contain.
    Where the specification prints an algorithm or a worked example, the case
    carries the document&rsquo;s own version of it rather than restating ours:
    E.2&rsquo;s XXTEA, E.3.1&rsquo;s CRC-24, G.1.6&rsquo;s exponential encoder
    and the example tables of G.1.7 to G.1.9.
  </p>
  <p>
    104 cases, of which <strong>64 run and pass</strong> and 40 are skipped. A
    skipped case is written as the clause requires it and skipped over a
    one-line note saying why, so what this device does not do is a list rather
    than a silence. Fourteen of the skips are clauses with nothing executable in
    them, such as the block diagram and the OSI table, and two are duties of the
    manufacturer rather than of the device. The rest is the honest part:
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Clause</th><th>What is missing, and why</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>G.2, G.2.1 to G.2.11 &mdash; Status payload</th>
          <td>not transmitted. It is the next protocol work, and soft versioning (B.5) waits on it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>C.3 to C.3.2 &mdash; O-band LDR</th>
          <td>not received. One radio, and the second is already spent on the two M-band channels and the O-band HDR uplink slot.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>E.4 to E.4.2 &mdash; secure signature</th>
          <td>no Ed25519 key is held, generated or transmitted.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>G.4, G.5 &mdash; FIS-B and Remote Identification</th>
          <td>neither received nor sent. RID is a UAV payload this aircraft device has no use for.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>D.3 &mdash; media access</th>
          <td>a deliberate deviation, not an omission: no listen-before-talk. The burst instant is drawn at random inside the slot and re-keyed every second, and the 1&nbsp;% hourly duty cycle is the channel-access route. The argument is written out in <code>core/timing/README.md</code>, and none of the reference implementations cancels a burst either.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>G.1.17 &mdash; quality indicators</th>
          <td>this firmware transmits SIL 1 and a DOP-derived NIC, which was the Issue 1 reading. Issue 2 asks an uncertified device with no protection bound for 0 in both. Being aligned.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note">
    The whole list, with the note on every skip, is the suite&rsquo;s own output:
    <code>build/skyblip_tests --no-skip --test-case=&quot;ADS-L*&quot;</code>.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">The address</h2>
  <h3>What we do today, and what an entry would change.</h3>

  <p>
    Today a skyBlip has no range to draw from, so it mints its own: the 24-bit
    address is derived from the MCU serial number and transmitted under an AMT
    entry in the self-minted group, 0 to 4. Because that space is shared with
    every other tracker that derives its identity the same way, the device moves
    off the prefixes known to be crowded &mdash; 0xD0, 0xDD to 0xDF and 0x11
    &mdash; and refuses the two addresses that mean &ldquo;none&rdquo; and
    &ldquo;dead SPI read&rdquo;, 0x000000 and 0xFFFFFF.
  </p>
  <p>
    F.2.2 offers a manufacturer a page of the AMT and an 8-bit prefix inside it,
    and asks that the remaining 16 bits be filled densely. That shape assumes a
    production line: someone issues address n+1 to the next unit and writes it
    down. This firmware is GPL-3.0 and builds from source, so a share of the
    fleet is assembled by its owner, flashed at a kitchen table, and passes
    through no counter we could run. Such a device mints its address the only
    way an offline device can, from the MCU serial number, and 16 bits are too
    few to absorb that: derived addresses collide at even odds by about 300
    devices.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the application is for one AMT entry with the structuring of F.2.2
    waived: 6 bits of AMT and 24 bits of address, no 8-bit prefix in between. We
    are asking for a deviation and say so plainly, but it is one the table
    already grants three times over. Entries 6, 7 and 8 are flat 24-bit spaces,
    and <strong>OGN-Tracker at entry 7</strong> is a fleet of exactly this
    shape: devices built rather than bought, each deriving its identity locally,
    with a public database resolving who is who. Assigned that way, the entry
    costs the table one page out of 55 and ends the guesswork for every skyBlip
    ever built, not only for the ones we ship.
  </p>
  <p>
    The entry we ask for is <strong>58</strong>, manufacturers page 49, for one
    reason only: 58 reads as SB. It is the single piece of vanity in this
    application and the registry should overrule it without hesitation, since a
    page issued out of order is a favour and the exception above is the part
    that matters. Any entry in 9 to 63 works for us.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    A 24-bit space does not make collisions impossible either. It makes them
    rare enough to settle one at a time: about 4,800 derived addresses before
    even odds of a single colliding pair. What settles them is a register, which
    is the first of the two answers in the question below.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="pledge">
  <h2 class="kicker">One question</h2>
  <h3>Where does an aircraft&rsquo;s callsign go?</h3>
  <p>
    In VFR the identity everyone uses &mdash; the pilot on the radio, the club,
    the EFB, the field &mdash; is the registration: F-GXXX on a certified
    aircraft, an <strong>F-J callsign</strong> on a French microlight. It
    belongs to the aircraft for its life. It is not a per-flight item like an
    airline flight number, and a receiver that could show it would be showing
    the traffic the way a pilot already thinks about it.
  </p>
  <p>
    We think this matters in the air rather than on paper. A pilot hears
    &ldquo;F-JABC, joining downwind for 25&rdquo; on the frequency, and what
    turns that call into an aircraft he can look for is a display showing the
    same callsign on the target: the radio tells him who, the screen tells him
    where, and only the callsign joins the two. Without it he has a call on one
    side and an anonymous return on the other, and traffic a pilot cannot
    correlate is traffic he ends up looking for in the wrong part of the sky.
  </p>
  <p>
    Issue 2 carries no field for it. The Traffic payload (G.1) has none, the
    Status payload (G.2) has none, and the only identity on the air is the
    30-bit sender address of F.2.2: 6 bits of AMT and 24 bits of address. That
    address is ours rather than the aircraft&rsquo;s, and nothing in the frame
    ties it to the registration on the fuselage. The aircraft this equipment
    addresses are the ones carrying no other identity on the air: a French
    microlight, a paraglider, a paramotor.
  </p>
  <p>
    So we would like the registry&rsquo;s view on which of the two answers it
    intends, because we would rather implement the one that becomes the
    standard:
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Answer</th><th>What it would take</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>A published address-to-registration database</th>
          <td>the way the OGN device database resolves FLARM and OGN addresses today. We would publish our assigned range, let owners bind their address to their registration, and expose the mapping under an open license.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>A callsign payload</th>
          <td>a low-rate broadcast payload carrying the registration, transmitted every few seconds rather than every second. A new payload type has to be approved by the governing body (F.2.5), which is why this is a question to the registry and not a proposal we would ship on our own.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Either is a small amount of work here. Neither is worth doing twice, and a
    manufacturer that invents its own answer is how a standard stops being one.
  </p>
</section>

